    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ida-of-bernicia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ida-of-bernicia.jpg" alt="Ida of Bernicia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ida-of-bernicia">Ida of Bernicia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ida_-_John_Speed.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Speed, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Northumbria, the land of the people north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#humber">Humber</a>, was formed from two Anglian kingdoms: Bernicia, centred on the coastal fortress of Bamburgh, whose dynasty traced its descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ida-of-bernicia">Ida</a> in the mid 6th century, and Deira, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yorkshire-wolds">Yorkshire Wolds</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#york">York</a>. They were first joined around 604 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith of Bernicia</a>, who defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aedan-mac-gabrain">Áedán mac Gabráin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-degsastan">Battle of Degsastan</a> in 603 and the Britons near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-chester">Chester</a> about 615. After Æthelfrith's death at the River Idle in 616, the Deiran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#edwin-of-northumbria">Edwin</a> ruled both kingdoms and was baptised at York by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paulinus-of-york">Paulinus</a> in 627, but his death at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-hatfield-chase">Battle of Hatfield Chase</a> in 633 split them again. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald</a> reunited them after his victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-heavenfield">Heavenfield</a> in 634. His brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oswiu">Oswiu</a>, king of Bernicia from 642, struggled with Deiran rivals, had King Oswine killed in 651 and finally made the union lasting after he defeated and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#penda-of-mercia">Penda of Mercia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-winwaed">Battle of the Winwaed</a> in 655; the kingdom is dated here from that mid-650s reunion. From then Northumbria stretched from the Humber to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#firth-of-forth">Firth of Forth</a>, absorbing the British kingdoms of Elmet, Rheged and Gododdin and reaching west to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#irish-sea">Irish Sea</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#york"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/york.jpg" alt="York" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#york">York</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:York_Minster_(2797690).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FEGreene, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Northumbria was a land of upland pasture and lowland farming, organised in large estates, or shires, that supplied food renders to the king and his nobles. Royal centres such as Bamburgh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yeavering">Yeavering</a>, where excavations revealed a great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timber">timber</a> hall and assembly structure, and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#york">York</a> formed the hubs of power. York, the old Roman city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eboracum">Eboracum</a>, became the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">bishop</a> in 627 and of an archbishop from 735, and its trading quarter developed into a port with links to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frisia">Frisia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rhineland">Rhineland</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#christianity">Christianity</a> came from two directions: Roman missionaries under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paulinus-of-york">Paulinus</a> converted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#edwin-of-northumbria">Edwin</a>'s court, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald</a> brought monks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iona">Iona</a> under Aidan, who founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> in 635. Monasteries became the richest institutions of the kingdom. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#benedict-biscop">Benedict Biscop</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#monkwearmouth-jarrow-abbey">Wearmouth</a> in 674 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jarrow">Jarrow</a> in the early 680s, with books, glaziers and masons brought from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gaul">Gaul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hild-of-whitby">Abbess Hild</a> ruled the double monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#whitby-abbey">Whitby</a>. These houses held large endowments of land and produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscripts</a> on an extraordinary scale; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#codex-amiatinus">Codex Amiatinus</a> alone required the skins of hundreds of calves. Kings issued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> coins from around 700, and in the 9th century small base-metal coins known as stycas circulated in large numbers.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-winwaed"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-winwaed.jpg" alt="Battle of the Winwaed" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-winwaed">Battle of the Winwaed</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Penda_of_Mercia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Violetriga, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Northumbrian power was won by war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aethelfrith-of-northumbria">Æthelfrith</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#edwin-of-northumbria">Edwin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oswiu">Oswiu</a> each held a wide overlordship backed by royal war bands, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bede">Bede</a> counts Edwin, Oswald and Oswiu among the kings who held <em>imperium</em> over the southern English. Oswiu's victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-winwaed">Winwaed</a> in 655 gave him control of Mercia for three years and made him the strongest king in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ecgfrith-of-northumbria">Ecgfrith</a> extended rule over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#picts">Picts</a> and established a bishopric at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abercorn">Abercorn</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#firth-of-forth">Forth</a>, but lost Lindsey after defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aethelred-of-mercia">Æthelred of Mercia</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-the-trent">Battle of the Trent</a> in 679. In 684 his army raided Brega in Ireland, and in 685 he led an invasion of Pictland that ended in disaster at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-dun-nechtain">Battle of Nechtansmere</a>, where the Pictish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bridei-son-of-beli">Bridei son of Beli</a> killed him and destroyed his army. Northumbrian expansion northwards ended, and the Picts, Scots and some Britons regained their independence. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eadberht-of-northumbria">Eadberht</a> (737–758) took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kyle-ayrshire">Kyle</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ayrshire">Ayrshire</a> and in 756, with the Pictish king Óengus, forced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-strathclyde">Britons of Dumbarton</a> to submit, but lost most of his army shortly afterwards. In the 9th century Northumbria faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vikings">Vikings</a>, and in 867 its rival kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#osberht-of-northumbria">Osberht</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aella-of-northumbria">Ælla</a> were killed trying to retake York from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#saint-cuthbert"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-cuthbert.jpg" alt="Saint Cuthbert" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#saint-cuthbert">Saint Cuthbert</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuthbert_discovers_piece_of_timber_-_Life_of_St._Cuthbert_(late_12th_C),_f.45v_-_BL_Yates_Thompson_MS_26.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bede, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The king ruled with a council of nobles and churchmen, travelling between royal estates and relying on reeves to collect renders and enforce justice. Royal power depended heavily on the church: kings founded and endowed monasteries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bishop">bishops</a> such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#wilfrid">Wilfrid of York</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saint-cuthbert">Cuthbert of Lindisfarne</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ecgbert-of-york">Ecgberht of York</a> were political figures in their own right. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egbert-of-wessex">Ecgberht</a>, brother of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eadberht-of-northumbria">Eadberht</a>, secured the archiepiscopal pallium in 735, and for two decades the brothers ruled kingdom and church together. In 664 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oswiu">Oswiu</a> presided over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#synod-of-whitby">Synod of Whitby</a>, which decided that the Northumbrian church would follow Roman rather than Irish practice in calculating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#easter-controversy">Easter</a>, a choice with lasting consequences for the English church. Royal succession was open to several branches of the Bernician dynasty and to rival families, and after the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aldfrith-of-northumbria">Aldfrith</a> in 704 or 705 it became extremely unstable. In the 8th century most kings were deposed, exiled or murdered, and several entered monasteries. This instability weakened the kingdom just as other powers grew. From the late 9th century the old kingdom was divided: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vikings">Viking</a> kings ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#york">York</a> and the south, while English high-reeves or earls based at Bamburgh governed the north between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#river-tees">Tees</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#river-tweed">Tweed</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#ruthwell-cross"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ruthwell-cross.jpg" alt="Ruthwell Cross" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#ruthwell-cross">Ruthwell Cross</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ruthwell_Cross_-_geograph.org.uk_-_939546.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lairich Rig, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Northumbria's lasting importance lies in its culture. The monasteries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#monkwearmouth-jarrow-abbey">Wearmouth-Jarrow</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#whitby-abbey">Whitby</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hexham">Hexham</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ripon">Ripon</a> created the so-called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#northumbrian-renaissance">Northumbrian Renaissance</a>, which blended Irish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> traditions. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lindisfarne-gospels">Lindisfarne Gospels</a>, made around 700, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#codex-amiatinus">Codex Amiatinus</a>, the oldest surviving complete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> Bible, taken towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> by Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ceolfrith">Ceolfrith</a> in 716, are among the finest books of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. The stone crosses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ruthwell-cross">Ruthwell</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bewcastle-cross">Bewcastle</a> carry some of the most ambitious sculpture of the period, and the Ruthwell Cross bears runic verses related to the poem <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#the-dream-of-the-rood">The Dream of the Rood</a></em>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bede">Bede</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jarrow">Jarrow</a> (c. 673–735) wrote the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ecclesiastical-history-of-the-english-people">Ecclesiastical History of the English People</a></em>, completed in 731, the foundation of English historical writing, and his works on time-reckoning helped spread the practice of dating events from the birth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jesus">Christ</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caedmon">Cædmon</a>'s hymn, composed at Whitby, is among the earliest recorded poems in English. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a>, educated at the cathedral school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#york">York</a>, became the leading scholar at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>'s court and shaped the Carolingian revival of learning. Through these men and books Northumbria influenced the intellectual life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a>, Ireland and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frankish-kingdom">Frankish kingdoms</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-dun-nechtain"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-dun-nechtain.jpg" alt="Battle of Dun Nechtain" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-dun-nechtain">Battle of Dun Nechtain</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pictish_Stone_at_Aberlemno_Church_Yard_-_Battle_Scene_Detail.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Greenshed, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Political weakness followed the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-dun-nechtain">Nechtansmere</a> in 685 and grew through the 8th century, as rival families fought over the throne. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vikings">Viking</a> raid on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lindisfarne">Lindisfarne</a> in June 793, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a> lamented in letters from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francia">Francia</a>, opened a new era of attacks. In 829 the Northumbrians submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egbert-of-wessex">Ecgberht of Wessex</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dore-south-yorkshire">Dore</a>, though only briefly. The decisive blow came in 866–867, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> seized York; the kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#osberht-of-northumbria">Osberht</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aella-of-northumbria">Ælla</a>, who had ended their own quarrel to fight together, were killed attacking the city in March 867. The Vikings set up dependent English kings north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#river-tyne">Tyne</a>, and in 876 their leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#halfdan-ragnarsson">Halfdan</a> shared out the lands of southern Northumbria among his followers, founding the Viking Kingdom of York. English rulers held out at Bamburgh as high-reeves or earls. In 927 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aethelstan">Æthelstan</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#york">York</a> and received the submission of the northern rulers, but Norse and Hiberno-Norse kings repeatedly regained the city in the following decades. The last of them, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eric-bloodaxe">Eric Bloodaxe</a>, was expelled in 954, traditionally dying at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#stainmore">Stainmore</a>, and York and the rest of Northumbria were absorbed into the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#england">England</a>, with the north governed by earls of the house of Bamburgh.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Northumbria was the largest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdom of the 7th century and the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#northumbrian-renaissance">Northumbrian Renaissance</a>, whose monasteries produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bede">Bede</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lindisfarne-gospels">Lindisfarne Gospels</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#codex-amiatinus">Codex Amiatinus</a>, shaping learning across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francia">Francia</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vikings">Viking</a> conquest divided it and it passed to the English crown in 954.</em></p>

